    <header class="page-hero">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="kicker">Typical, not guaranteed</p>
        <h1>A step-by-step timeline</h1>
        <p class="lede">These are ranges I treat as ordinary when people cooperate and the file is clean. A death in the title, a possession-only lot, or a seller who travels can blow past all of them. Nothing here is a promise of a closing date.</p>
      </div>
    </header>
    <section class="section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <ol class="steps">
          <li class="step">
            <h2>1. Intake and a written brief</h2>
            <p class="when">Often a few days to two weeks</p>
            <p>Budget, months in Peru, climate, and whether renting first is the real next step. The <a href="quiz.html">quiz</a> can happen the same day. The <a href="contact.html">form</a> is the brief.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="step">
            <h2>2. Search and a shortlist</h2>
            <p class="when">A few weeks to a few months</p>
            <p>Portals, signs, and conversations. Off-market takes longer and wastes less money. I say when a region needs a local contact instead of me.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="step">
            <h2>3. Scouting</h2>
            <p class="when">Several days on the ground for a focused trip</p>
            <p>More than one hour of the day. A rainy-season check can be a separate trip weeks or months later. Remote video can happen inside this step when you are not here.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="step">
            <h2>4. Documents</h2>
            <p class="when">Often one to four weeks if the seller cooperates</p>
            <p><span lang="es">Certificado literal</span>, <span lang="es">gravámenes</span>, municipal parameters, who must sign, taxes owed. Succession and possession files are not “a few weeks.” They are a different project. Your lawyer reads them. I do not.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="step">
            <h2>5. Arras or a separation agreement</h2>
            <p class="when">Days, once the terms are agreed</p>
            <p>Only after the conditions are written. A deposit in a chat is not this step.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="step">
            <h2>6. Minuta and the notary’s calendar</h2>
            <p class="when">Often days to a few weeks</p>
            <p>The lawyer’s draft, then scheduling the <span lang="es">escritura pública</span>. Remote buyers add the <span lang="es">poder</span> here or earlier: consulate or apostille, which itself can take weeks.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="step">
            <h2>7. Signing</h2>
            <p class="when">A day, once the minuta is ready</p>
            <p>Before the notary. You or your properly scoped attorney-in-fact. The notary is not your advocate.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="step">
            <h2>8. SUNARP</h2>
            <p class="when">Often several weeks, sometimes longer</p>
            <p>Registration, and any observations the registry raises. You are not finished when the pen is capped.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="step">
            <h2>9. Utilities, and the first month</h2>
            <p class="when">Days to a couple of months</p>
            <p>Accounts in your name, a caretaker if you are leaving, and the unglamorous list on the <a href="after.html">after page</a>.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="note">A clean urban purchase, after you have chosen the property, often lands around one to three months. I would not quote that range for rural land, an estate, or anything inside a doubtful border measurement.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
